<h1>Case work and proof</h1>
<p class="lede">We don't publish any client's name without their express permission, and right now we don't have it. So instead of names, here is the work.</p>

<section>
  <h2>A recent project</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A full audit of a web product and its browser extension, across web and Windows 11 environments. Manual evaluation with NVDA and keyboard-only navigation against WCAG 2.2 Level AA, EN 301 549 and the European Accessibility Act. Two people from the team ran it.</p>
    <p>The main finding was not a long list of different problems. It was the same one, repeated screen after screen, in a component reused across the whole product. That changes the client's job completely: it is not a hundred fixes but one done properly. Counting findings would have produced an alarming number and a wrong idea of the real effort.</p>
    <p>The delivery was a 29-page report with findings prioritized by severity, cognitive accessibility recommendations, and a draft accessibility statement in Spanish and English.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>What you actually get</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>An accessibility report is not a list of errors dumped out of a tool. This is what every report that leaves here contains:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The scope reviewed: which pages, which journeys and which components.</li>
      <li>The method and test environment: which screen readers, which browsers and which version of the standard.</li>
      <li>Each finding with the WCAG criterion it fails, its level, the steps to reproduce it and an acceptance criterion to close it.</li>
      <li>The full criteria table, including what does pass, which is what an accessibility statement rests on.</li>
      <li>The proposed fix, not just the problem flagged.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The <a href="/en/services/">service details</a> explain which service includes what, and in which format it can be delivered.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>You can audit this site too</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>It is the easiest proof to check and it doesn't depend on taking our word for it: go through this site with a keyboard, with a screen reader, or at 400% zoom, and see whether it holds up. Our <a href="/en/accessibility-statement/">accessibility statement</a> says what we meet, how we tested it, and what we don't claim.</p>
  </div>
</section>

